Description: Hold no logout for unsaved documents which return with the session, and save the session when it ends.
 Every window with an unsaved document asked GNOME to hold a logout, so GNOME Shell's logout dialog listed Text Editor
 as having unsaved work, although Text Editor saves unsaved documents as drafts and restores them at its next start.
 When the session of the desktop ends, GTK quits Text Editor, which then saved nothing: the next start restored the
 state of the last auto-save, which is not marked as shut down, as after a crash, without the text typed since.
 .
 While Text Editor restores its session, unsaved documents now hold no logout, and the session is saved at shutdown
 when nothing requested a save for shutdown before, as closing the last window and quitting do. Shutdown waits for
 that save and for the saves still running, for at most 5 seconds. A session which does not restore its pages, with
 restore-session off or with --ignore-session, still holds the logout for unsaved documents, and saves nothing at
 shutdown, so that the next start still restores its unsaved documents as after a crash.
Author: Mitar
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-08
